# NOETHERVIM: KEYMAP NAMESPACES

NoetherVim binds more keys than anyone memorizes, and you are not meant to
memorize them. Every binding lives under a prefix, and each prefix owns one
kind of action. Learn the six prefixes and the editor tells you the rest.

This lesson takes about ten minutes. Nothing in it can damage your setup:
a tutorial buffer is never written to disk, so edit it freely.

Table of contents:

- [How this lesson works](*howto*)
- [SearchLeader, for finding things](*searchleader*)
- [Leader, for acting on the editor](*leader*)
- [Square brackets, for moving through lists](*brackets*)
- [Bracket-o, for turning options on and off](*options*)
- [Ctrl-W, for windows and panels](*windows*)
- [Z, for closing things](*closing*)
- [The prefixes this lesson skips](*rest*)
- [Recap](*recap*)

## HOW THIS LESSON WORKS *howto*

Two mechanics first, then the content.

**Exercise lines check themselves.** A line marked ✗ in the left margin is
waiting on you, and becomes ✓ the moment its text is right. Fix the line
below by changing `wrong` into `right`.

this line is wrong

**`>>` runs a command for you.** Put the cursor on an indented command and
press `>>`. In an ordinary buffer `>>` indents a line; inside a tutorial it
executes one, so you never have to retype what you are reading about.

Try it on this one, which prints everything `:NoetherVim` can do:

~~~ cmd
    :NoetherVim
~~~

That output goes to a notification. TIP if it clears before you finish
reading, `<Space>fn` brings back the list of recent notifications.

## SEARCHLEADER, FOR FINDING THINGS *searchleader*

SearchLeader is `<Space>` by default. It owns one job: **finding something
and going there**. Nothing under it changes your text, your files or your
options, which is what makes it safe to explore by pressing keys.

Press `<Space>` now and wait a moment without pressing anything else.

A panel lists every group underneath it. `f` is find, `g` is grep, `l` is
LSP, `c` is configuration, `d` is diagnostics, `G` is git. Press `f` and
the panel narrows to the file pickers. Press `<Esc>` to back out.

That pattern is the whole skill: press a prefix, read, and keep going.

Two worth knowing right now. `<Space>ff` finds a file by name, and
`<Space>?` opens a list of every keymap currently active, grouped the way
this lesson groups them. Run the guide and press `q` to come back:

~~~ cmd
    :NoetherVim keymap-guide
~~~

Fill in the two-letter key that fuzzy-finds a file by name:

The find-a-file picker is ___

NOTE this lesson writes `<Space>` because that is the default. If you
rebind SearchLeader, every `<Space>` here means your key instead. See
[the SearchLeader option](noethervim-mapsearchleader).

## LEADER, FOR ACTING ON THE EDITOR *leader*

Leader is `\` by default, and it is the counterpart to SearchLeader: where
SearchLeader finds, **Leader acts**. Formatting a buffer, running the file,
yanking to the system clipboard, starting a debug session.

Press `\` now and wait. Read the panel, then press `<Esc>`.

The split matters more than the individual keys. When you want to *go*
somewhere, reach for `<Space>`. When you want something *done*, reach for
`\`. A key that changes your buffer will never be under `<Space>`.

## SQUARE BRACKETS, FOR MOVING THROUGH LISTS *brackets*

`[` and `]` move backward and forward through whatever kind of thing you
name next. `]b` is the next buffer, `[b` the previous one; `]t` and `[t`
walk tabs; `]d` and `[d` walk diagnostics; `]q` and `[q` walk the quickfix
list.

The direction is always the same. `[` goes back, `]` goes forward, for
every kind of list, so a list you have never used still moves the way you
expect.

## BRACKET-O, FOR TURNING OPTIONS ON AND OFF *options*

Adding `o` to the pair changes it from movement to a switch: `[o` turns an
option **on**, `]o` turns it **off**. The letter after names the option.

Try it. Press `]on` and the line numbers disappear; press `[on` and they
come back.

Now `[ox`, which turns on a crosshair through the cursor, and `]ox` to turn
it off again. Others follow the same shape: `w` is wrap, `s` is spell, `h`
is search highlighting, `l` is whitespace characters.

Fill in the half of the pair that turns an option on:

The prefix that turns an option on is ___

The full list is in [the option toggle reference](noethervim-option-toggles).

## CTRL-W, FOR WINDOWS AND PANELS *windows*

Everything that makes, moves, sizes or fills a window is under `<C-w>`,
extending the prefix Vim already uses for window commands.

Walk through it once. Press `<C-w>v` to split this window in two, then
`<C-w>h` and `<C-w>l` to move between the halves. Leave the extra one open
for the next section.

Panels live here too, because a panel is a window: `<C-w><C-o>` opens the
file browser, `<C-w>l` the plugin manager, `<C-w>t` a terminal.

## Z, FOR CLOSING THINGS *closing*

`Z` closes things, and its keys form a grid rather than a list of initials.
The row says how much you are willing to lose, the column says what you are
closing. Vim's own `ZZ` and `ZQ` sit in the first column.

                    this window   everything    this buffer
    force             ZQ            ZW            ZE
    refuse if dirty   ZA            ZS            ZD
    save first        ZZ            ZX            ZC

Close the split you opened a moment ago with `ZA`. It refuses if the buffer
has unsaved changes, which is the row you want almost always.

`ZW` is the one to remember for later: it leaves Neovim from anywhere.

## THE PREFIXES THIS LESSON SKIPS *rest*

Two more exist, and both need a real file to be worth practicing on.

`g` covers going to things a language server knows about: `gd` to a
definition, `gR` to references, `go` to an outline. It needs an attached
language server, so it does nothing in this buffer.

`<LocalLeader>` is `,` and holds actions belonging to one filetype, such as
compiling a LaTeX document. It is always buffer-local, so the keys under it
change as you move between files.

## RECAP *recap*

Fill in the prefix that owns each action. Each answer goes at the end of
its line, after the existing space.

Fuzzy-finding a file lives under: ___
Turning line numbers back on lives under: ___
Splitting the window lives under: ___
Closing this window and nothing else is: ___

When all four are ✓, you are done.

## WHERE TO GO NEXT

If modes and motions are still new, the two tutorials Neovim ships are the
better next step: [lesson one](@tutor:vim-01-beginner) covers moving,
editing and saving, and [lesson two](@tutor:vim-02-beginner) continues it.

To read the namespaces as reference rather than a walkthrough, see
[the keymap chapter](noethervim-keymaps). To see every key live in your own
install, with a jump to the line that defined it, press `<Space>?`.
